> # ================================================================
> # Task 38 - SHARP: a new outlier detector, built from the literature
> #           and from this project's own failure analysis
> # Date: Sep 15, 2026
> #
> # THE REQUEST (2026-09-15)
> #
> #   "Try out new ways of detecting outliers. Read new papers ... use
> #    their ideas to invent something new that might work."
> #
> # WHY A NEW METHOD, AND WHY A NEW YARDSTICK
> #
> #   Tasks 24-37 found five separate things wrong with per-CpG outlier
> #   flags on this cohort:
> #     1. single-CpG cutoffs sit inside the array's measurement noise at
> #        compressed (L, H) sites                           (Tasks 22, 32)
> #     2. with n = 53 a cohort's own tail is an order statistic, not an
> #        estimate                                                (Task 13)
> #     3. non-uniquely mapping probes manufacture flags           (Task 36)
> #     4. whole arrays (N14, N15, N17) are offset, most visibly on type II
> #        probes, and dominate every burden ranking          (Tasks 36, 37)
> #     5. a flag is "extreme" but not necessarily "large"   (Tasks 26, 32)
> #
> #   The repo's benchmark so far scores stability under added noise. That
> #   rewards flagging less, and it has no ground truth. This task adds the
> #   yardstick used by ramr, OUTRIDER and METAFORA: inject artificial
> #   epimutations of known size into the real data and measure recall,
> #   next to the false calls a method makes on the untouched data.
> #
> # IDEAS BORROWED, AND WHERE EACH GOES
> #
> #   Zhou et al. 2017 (probe masks)        -> step 1, drop M_general probes
> #   RCP / ENmix (Niu et al. 2016)         -> step 2, remove each array's
> #                                            design-type offset
> #   OUTRIDER (Brechtmann 2018), METAFORA  -> step 3, remove hidden factors
> #   (2026)                                   before scoring; number chosen
> #                                            by injected outliers (OUTRIDER)
> #                                            and by Gavish-Donoho (METAFORA)
> #   limma eBayes (Smyth 2004)             -> step 4, shrink each CpG's scale
> #                                            toward CpGs like it
> #   Task 32 measured noise; Borealis      -> step 4, scale never below the
> #                                            platform's noise; "too small to
> #                                            call" class
> #   moderated t (limma); BH FDR           -> step 5, p-values and FDR
> #   epimutacions, ramr, Garg et al. 2020  -> step 6, >= 3 CpGs within 1 kb
> #   SEM reliability (Markov et al. 2024)  -> motivation for 4-6: single-CpG
> #                                            calls at low-variance CpGs
> #                                            replicate worst
> #
> # THE METHOD: SHARP
> #   S hrunken scale     H armonised arrays     A djusted for hidden factors
> #   R egion-aware       P robe-masked
> #
> #   1. mask    drop probes with Zhou M_general = TRUE
> #   2. harmonise  for each sample, each probe design (I-red, I-green, II)
> #              and each tenth of the cohort-median range, subtract the
> #              sample's median deviation. Removes whole-array offsets;
> #              a focal event touches too few probes to move a median of
> #              thousands.
> #   3. adjust  remove the top k principal components of the residual
> #              matrix (sample space, 53 x 53, so it is exact and cheap)
> #   4. scale   per-CpG MAD of the adjusted residuals, shrunk toward the
> #              median of CpGs with the same design and median bin
> #              (limma's moment estimator), floored at the Task 32 noise
> #              level for the CpG's state
> #   5. test    z = residual / scale, p from a t distribution with the
> #              moderated degrees of freedom, BH q-values over every cell
> #   6. call    SHARP.cpg:    q <= 0.05 AND |adjusted delta-beta| >= 0.10
> #              SHARP.region: seed-and-extend. Runs of >= 3 consecutive
> #              CpGs (each gap <= 1 kb) with p <= 0.01, |d| >= 0.05, same
> #              sign, containing at least one SHARP.cpg call, mean |d| >=
> #              0.10. The extension threshold is what lets a region call
> #              recover CpGs that are individually too weak.
> #
> # COMPARED AGAINST (all computable from local data)
> #   ext.recon         OutlierMeth ext flags, thresholds reconstructed from
> #                     the Task 13 flag matrix (bracket midpoint; where a site
> #                     has no flag on a side, the cohort max/min, which is
> #                     the most permissive threshold consistent with the
> #                     observed flags)
> #   ext.recon.floor   the same + |beta - cohort median| >= 0.10 (Task 26/32)
> #   SEM.3IQR          Gentilini et al. 2015: beyond Q1 - 3 IQR / Q3 + 3 IQR
> #   bio.stat.dev      Task 30: state-pooled 96th percentile of deviation
> #   epimut.style      inspired by epimutacions' quantile method: beyond the
> #                     leave-one-out 0.5 / 99.5 % quantile, |d| >= 0.15, and
> #                     >= 3 such CpGs within 1 kb. Not a reimplementation.
> #
> # EVALUATION
> #   A. two independent spike-in sets injected into the real Alive Normal
> #      matrix, only at unmasked probes: 600 single-CpG and 300 regional
> #      (3-6 CpGs within 1 kb) events at each mixing fraction pi in
> #      {0.10, 0.20, 0.35, 0.50}. hyper: b' = b + pi (1 - b); hypo:
> #      b' = b (1 - pi) - a clonal fraction pi fully (un)methylated.
> #      Direction follows the state (L/LM up, H/HM down, M/R random).
> #      Set A chooses k; set B scores everything.
> #   B. per method, at its own default:   recall by realised |delta-beta|,
> #      region-event recall (>= 50% of its CpGs), background calls per
> #      sample on clean data, share contrary to state, burden of the three
> #      type-II-shifted arrays relative to the median sample, stability
> #      (signed Jaccard, clean vs clean + N(0, 0.01)).
> #   C. at a matched false-call budget: each method's continuous score is
> #      thresholded so that the CLEAN data give 0.1% calls; recall is then
> #      read off the spiked data. The fair comparison.
> #   D. ablation: SHARP with each step removed.
> #   E. real data, both tissues: calls per sample; what happens to
> #      N14/N15/N17 and T14-T17; the Task 36 sites.
> #
> # OUTPUT (all aggregates; no individual value at a named CpG)
> #   Results/Task38_KSelection.csv, Task38_MethodScores.csv,
> #   Task38_RecallByEffect.csv, Task38_Ablation.csv,
> #   Task38_SampleBurden.csv, Task38_Task36Sites.csv
> #   Results/Fig16_SHARP_Recall.png, Fig17_SHARP_Budget.png,
> #   Results/Fig18_SHARP_Ablation.png
> # ================================================================
> 
